(* ===================================================================== *)
(* Sassembly T1 — LEXICAL grammar, FROZEN v0.1.                          *)
(* Task B-079a. Gate B11's prerequisite.                                 *)
(*                                                                        *)
(* Frozen means the T1 front end (B-080) may depend on this shape.        *)
(* Changing a production after this point is an ADR, not an edit.         *)
(*                                                                        *)
(* AMENDED 2026-08-28 by ADR-0017 (`F-004f2`): `token` gains `string`,     *)
(* which is what the note at the foot of this file asked for. It was added  *)
(* the way the line above requires.                                        *)
(*                                                                        *)
(* AMENDED 2026-08-28 by ADR-0018: `keyword` gains `यतिः` and `विवरम्`,      *)
(* two LAYOUT WORDS that are values. See the note in that production.      *)
(*                                                                        *)
(* AMENDED 2026-10-06 by ADR-0044 (D2), under the owner's exemption to the   *)
(* freeze: `string_open` gains `वर्णाष्टकम्`, a Devanagari-8 data literal. See *)
(* the note beside that production.                                       *)
(*                                                                        *)
(* ADDED 2026-08-28 by ADR-0019 (`D-002g`), OWNER-RATIFIED: the `embed`       *)
(* production — a derived table brought in BY NAME at assembly time. It is    *)
(* opened by `समावेशः`, WHICH THIS FILE ALREADY CARRIES as `include`. The      *)
(* owner ruled that the embed REUSE that keyword rather than that a new one   *)
(* be added, so ADR-0019 amends NO production: `keyword` is untouched, and    *)
(* `आनीतम्`, which the proposed draft would have added, is not a word of this   *)
(* language. `token` is untouched too — an embed's result is a `string`.      *)
(* ADDED 2026-08-29 by ADR-0026 (`D-002k`), OWNER-RATIFIED: `array_type`, a   *)
(* fixed-capacity array. It too amends NO production — it coins no word and   *)
(* reuses the ratified `index` pair with a `numeral` between the marks. See    *)
(* the note beside the production near the foot of this file.                 *)
(*                                                                        *)
(* AMENDED 2026-08-29 by ADR-0027 (`D-002l`): `keyword` gains `मण्डलम्`, and   *)
(* three productions are ADDED — `module_decl`, `import` and `qualified_name`. *)
(* The word is ADOPTED, not coined: `spec/lexicon.src.tsv:271` already carries *)
(* `मण्डलम् / module / keyword / attested`, and 19 of the 20 `.t1` sources in  *)
(* the tree already declare with it. See the `modules` section below.         *)
(*                                                                        *)
(*                                                                        *)
(* ADDED 2026-08-30 by ADR-0032 (`D-002h`): the EXPRESSION and OPERATOR       *)
(* section. Thirteen binary operators, one unary, and a nine-level precedence *)
(* ladder — all MEASURED from the `.t1` corpus the owner ruled canonical on    *)
(* 2026-08-29, not chosen. No production above it is amended.                 *)
(*                                                                        *)
(*                                                                        *)
(* MERGE NOTE 2026-08-31: two branches amended this header for two real     *)
(* reasons and neither is withdrawn, so both notes above are kept. The      *)
(* operator ADR was accepted on `origin/main` as ADR-0026 while this branch *)
(* had accepted a DIFFERENT ADR-0026, the fixed-capacity array; the same    *)
(* collision holds for 0027 and for 0028. THE NUMBERS STILL COLLIDE IN      *)
(* `docs/adr/` AS OF THIS COMMIT. The tests cite origin's numbers, so       *)
(* origin's keep them and this branch's three move; that is its own row.    *)
(* Until it lands, read the TASK ID, which is never ambiguous: `D-002k` and *)
(* `D-002l` are this branch's array and module ADRs, and `D-002h` is        *)
(* origin's operator, statement and lexicon work.                           *)
(*                                                                        *)
(* EVERY DEVIATION FROM THE FREEZE IS ADR-BACKED, which is what the line    *)
(* above requires. The COUNT that stood here is dropped rather than merged: *)
(* each side computed it against a different baseline -- one calls          *)
(* `unary_expr` amended, and `unary_expr` did not exist at the freeze at    *)
(* all -- so no single number is true of the union. Deriving one from the   *)
(* freeze gives 35 added, which is not what either sentence meant. The      *)
(* ADRs are the record; each production carries its own head.               *)
(*                                                                        *)
(* THE PHRASE STRUCTURE IS HERE AS OF B-079b, THE EXPRESSION GRAMMAR AS OF  *)
(* the operator ADR, AND `statement`/`type`/`call` AS OF ADR-0027 -- both   *)
(* task `D-002h`. What is left is declaration BODIES; see "what is STILL    *)
(* not frozen" below.                                                       *)
(* ===================================================================== *)

(* --- why this file stops where it does -------------------------------- *)

(* Two sources describe T1 and neither can be transcribed.
 *
 * Doc 02 §3.2 is a SKETCH, and says so: its `{ } ( ) , = + * →` are ASCII
 * "for legibility while the semantics are under discussion". Its normative
 * spelling is doc 15 §3.4–3.5.
 *
 * Doc 15 §3.1's table assigns those roles to Siddham and Vedic-extension
 * signs — `꣼ ꣸` for grouping, `꣺ ꣻ` for blocks, `ᳵ ᳶ` for indexing,
 * `꣹` for strings. **Task A-033 measured all seven at 0 of 27 Devanagari
 * faces.** ADR-0003 already acted on that measurement for T0, replacing the
 * string delimiter with the words `उक्तम् … इति`, and ratified exactly six
 * signs — `। ॥ ॰ ॱ ॱॱ ऽ` — because those are the ones that render.
 *
 * T0 needed one of the seven and could work around it. T1 needs grouping,
 * blocks and indexing as well, and there is no ratified sign for any of them.
 *
 * Doc 15 §15.3.3 says how to decide: a READABILITY STUDY, five readers, three
 * programs each, closure form against an ASCII control, measuring comprehension
 * time and error rate. Doc 15's own risk table names the danger it settles —
 * "operator-words make code unreadably verbose" — and says to carve sign
 * operators out of the Vedic block if the data says so.
 *
 * That study needs human readers. It is `A-063` and it is the owner's.
 * Freezing the phrase structure before it would be guessing at the most
 * consequential surface decision in the language, and this project does not
 * guess where it can measure.
 *
 * What IS decided is the lexical layer: every token below is spelled with
 * signs ADR-0003 ratified, or with words, and none of it waits on the study.
 *)

(* --- tokens ----------------------------------------------------------- *)

token          = keyword
               | type_name
               | identifier
               | numeral
               | string
               | comment ;

(* --- keywords --------------------------------------------------------- *)

(* Doc 02 §3.2's v0.1 set. Each is an ordinary Sanskrit word, so each is also
   a possible identifier — the reader must prefer the keyword, which is why
   they are enumerated here rather than recognised by shape. *)
keyword        = "वृत्तिः"        (* fn        *)
               | "प्रकारः"        (* type      *)
               | "संरचना"         (* struct    *)
               | "संघः"           (* union     *)
               | "गणना"           (* enum      *)
               | "चरः"            (* var       *)
               | "ध्रुवः"          (* const     *)
               | "यदि" | "अन्यथा"  (* if / else *)
               | "यावत्"          (* while     *)
               | "प्रत्येकम्"       (* for       *)
               | "भङ्गः"           (* break     *)
               | "अनुवर्तनम्"      (* continue  *)
               | "प्रत्यागमनम्"     (* return    *)
               | "विकल्पना"       (* switch    *)
               | "रक्षा"           (* defer     *)
               | "प्रयत्नः"        (* try       *)
               | "दोषः"           (* error     *)
               | "समयकृत"         (* comptime  *)
               | "परीक्षा"         (* test      *)
               | "सार्वजनिक"      (* pub       *)
               | "मण्डलम्"         (* module — ADR-0026, the DECLARING half of
                                     the pair below; see the `modules` section *)
               | "आयातः"          (* import    *)
               | "समावेशः"        (* include — AND the embed opener; see the
                                     `embed` production and ADR-0019 *)
               | "शून्यम्"         (* void      *)
               | "सत्यम्" | "असत्यम्" (* true / false *)
               | "रिक्तः"          (* null      *)
               | "स्वयम्"          (* self      *)
               | "असुरक्षितम्"     (* unsafe    *)

               (* ADR-0018, owner-ratified 2026-08-28. Two LAYOUT WORDS, and
                  they are VALUES rather than escapes: `यतिः` is U+000A and
                  `विवरम्` is U+0020 wherever a value may stand, and INSIDE
                  `उक्तम् … इति` each is simply its own akṣaras. That
                  distinction is guarded by exactly one test — decoding either
                  inside a literal fails `a_layout_word_inside_a_string_is_its_
                  own_letters` AND NOTHING ELSE, not even ADR-0017's suite.

                  `यतिः` is prosody's caesura and is not coined here: doc 17a
                  §3.3 already names renderer's line breaker `यतिः` for this
                  concept and `crates/renderer/src/yati.rs` implements it.
                  `विवरम्` (वि+√वृ) is the interstice. `अवकाशः`, the obvious
                  word for a space, IS TAKEN — `संरचना अवकाश` is the linker's
                  `Slot`, written 14 times across `samyojana.t1` and
                  `vishlesana.t1`, so reserving it would have silently changed
                  the meaning of code already in the tree. *)
               | "यतिः"           (* newline   *)
               | "विवरम्" ;        (* space     *)

(* ADR-0019 ADDS NO WORD TO THE LIST ABOVE, and the reason is the owner's
 * ruling of 2026-08-28.
 *
 * The proposed draft would have added `आनीतम्` ("brought in") as the embed
 * opener, and it gave two reasons for not reusing `समावेशः`. First, `समावेशः`
 * was written as a function name in `crates/sadhana-t1/src/encode.t1` —
 * `Slot::fits` — which is the hazard ADR-0018 refused `अवकाशः` over. Second,
 * `spec/directives.tsv:36` gives `समावेशः` as `.include`, "textual inclusion",
 * where an embed yields a file's BYTES as a VALUE.
 *
 * THE OWNER REFUSED BOTH, AND DIFFERENTLY FOR EACH.
 *
 * On the first: that occupancy was not a reason to spend a word, it was a
 * DEFECT. `समावेशः` is in the frozen list above, and this file says a keyword
 * "is also a possible identifier — the reader must prefer the keyword", so
 * `encode.t1` was already illegal and merely unnoticed, no front end having
 * been written to enforce the list. `B-112` renamed it — `Slot::fits` is
 * `अन्तर्भावः` now — and added the guard that matters more than the rename:
 * `no_t1_source_binds_a_frozen_keyword_as_a_name` in
 * `crates/sadhana-t1/tests/t1_sources.rs` reads THIS PRODUCTION, refuses any
 * `.t1` file that binds one of these words as a name, and cannot drift from the
 * grammar because it is not a copy of it. It found ten further collisions the
 * same day.
 *
 * On the second: one word carrying a T0 directive sense and a T1 sense is not
 * an anomaly here, it is the norm. Of the six directives at
 * `spec/directives.tsv:32`-`:39`, FIVE already do it — `स्थानम्` is `.space`
 * and the pointer type, `समम्` is `.equ` and the equality comparison, `यदि`
 * and `अन्यथा` are `.if`/`.else` and the keywords above, `अन्तः` is `.endif`
 * and the index close — and `समावेशः` was the only one that did not. The two
 * senses are told apart by the TIER (two grammars, two lexers, no source that
 * is both), by the WRAPPER (`॥ … ॥` for a directive, ADR-0012; `आरभ्य …
 * समाप्तम्` for the embed, ADR-0003) and by the ARGUMENT (a string, ADR-0013;
 * a bare identifier here). ADR-0019 says this at length.
 *
 * `आनीतम्` is therefore not a word of this language. *)

(* --- type names ------------------------------------------------------- *)

(* The same vocabulary T0 already uses for its व्याप्ति suffix (ADR-0010), so
   `ॱअ३२` in an instruction and `अ३२` in a declaration name one type. That
   agreement is the point: two tiers of one language, not two languages.

   `पाठः` BECAME `पाठ` ON 2026-08-31 BY ADR-0029, and the count that decides it
   is not the one ADR-0027 recorded. ADR-0027 wrote "`पाठ` appears 145 times
   against `पाठः`'s 39, both in type position"; measured again with POSITION
   taken into account — the token after an `annotation` or after `ददाति` — the
   corpus writes `पाठ` 27 times and `पाठः` 9, and the other 148 occurrences are
   parameter names, field names and values, which are `identifier` and not
   `type_name` at all. The direction is unchanged and the magnitude is a
   quarter of what was claimed. In type position `पाठ` is written in SIX of the
   fifteen files and all nine `पाठः` are in `utsarjana.t1` alone.

   BOTH SPELLINGS COULD NOT BE ADMITTED, and that is what forced a single
   choice rather than a widening. Doc 01 §4 rule 5 — one word, one sense; one
   sense, one word — is enforced by `no_concept_has_two_words` in
   `crates/sanskrit-text/tests/lexicon.rs`, and `spec/lexicon.src.tsv` holds no
   sense twice in 476 rows. A `type_name` naming two words for `string` would
   have made the lexicon unable to follow the grammar, which is the direction
   ADR-0028 fixed.

   `अक्षरम्` STAYS, unchanged and for ADR-0027's reason: it is written ZERO
   times in type position and so is unused rather than contradicted, and `अक्षर`
   is written zero times there too. There is no divergence to settle. *)
type_name      = integer_type | float_type | "बूल" | "अक्षरम्" | "पाठ" ;

(* ADR-0030 CONSIDERED WIDENING THIS AND REFUSED. The corpus writes `इ६४` 56
   times in type position — at that ONE width, never `इ८`/`इ१६`/`इ३२`/`इ१२८` —
   and this production cannot produce it. It is NOT admitted. `इ` is the Latin
   `i` of `i64` written in Devanagari, where `अंश`, `निर्ऋण` and `प्लव` are
   chosen words with derivations printed in doc 02 §2.5; the corpus annotates
   its own `इ६४` sites with Rust type names in English (`ir.t1:118` reads
   `॰ ConstInt's i64`), which is what a mechanical port leaves behind and not
   what a designed vocabulary needs. `tools/gen_levels.py`, the tree's OTHER
   writer of T1, already spells unsigned `न६४`/`न३२` 52 times and `इ` never.

   THE SAME PORT WROTE `अ` FOR UNSIGNED, 1322 TIMES: `encode.t1:212`'s
   `मूल्यम् ॱॱ अ६४` is `encode.rs:118`'s `value: u64`, and the `चिह्नितम् ॱॱ इ६४`
   beside it is the `let signed = value as i64` of `:132` hoisted into a
   parameter because T1 has no cast. Both directions are one defect with one
   cause, they live only in `crates/sadhana-t1/src/*.t1`, and ADR-0030
   authorises the remediation without performing it.

   AND `इ` IS NOT FREE TO TAKE: `hex_digit` below already spells `c` with it,
   `अ` being `a`, so `०षोड्इ६४` is a legal hexadecimal literal and admitting
   `इ` here would give one sign two senses inside this one grammar. *)
integer_type   = ( "अ" | "न" ) , type_width ;   (* अंश signed, निर्ऋण unsigned *)

(* ADR-0031 — AND NO CAST PRODUCTION IS ADDED EITHER, WHICH IS THE OTHER HALF
   OF THE NOTE ABOVE. ADR-0030 said the port hoisted `let signed = value as i64`
   into a parameter "because T1 has no cast" and left the gap named. It stays a
   gap ON PURPOSE: no T1 expression changes the type of what it evaluates —
   `type` is reachable from `binding` and the four type constructors and from no
   expression production at all — and a conversion routine could not be written
   in this language, since its body would need the very thing it exists to
   provide. Nor does arithmetic reach the case: `vishlesana.t1`'s उद्धरणम्
   converts by subtracting `१ वामसृ विस्तारः`, but only under
   `यदि विस्तारः न्यूनम् ६४`, because at sixty-four there is no bit above the
   sign to borrow from.

   WHAT STANDS IN ITS PLACE IS WHAT THE CORPUS ALREADY WRITES: the caller
   carries BOTH readings. `encode.t1:885` states it — "मूल्यानि and चिह्नितानि
   are the SAME numbers twice, unsigned and signed: अन्तर्भावः needs both, T1
   has no cast" — and the file writes the pair as two scalars at `:212`, as two
   parallel slices at `:893`, and unpacks the one into the other at `:916`–`:917`.
   Where the two readings are
   PROVABLY equal at every call, the pair is not written and the parameter
   follows the caller, with the guard that proves it in the file: `nidana.t1`'s
   पदार्थलेखनम् behind `यदि अङ्कम् अधिकम् ऋण१`, `utsarjana.t1`'s अङ्कचिह्नम्
   behind a remainder modulo `१०`. *)
float_type     = "प" , ( "३२" | "६४" ) ;        (* प्लव *)
type_width     = "८" | "१६" | "३२" | "६४" | "१२८" ;

(* `ᳵᳶ T` slice, `स्थानम् T` pointer, `सम्भाव्य T` optional and
   `दोषयुक्त T` error union are TYPE CONSTRUCTORS, not type names: they take
   an operand, so they are phrase structure and wait with the rest. The slice
   spelling in particular uses `ᳵ ᳶ`, which is one of the seven signs that
   render in no face. *)

(* --- identifiers, numerals, comments ---------------------------------- *)

(* Identical to T0, deliberately: one lexer serves both tiers, and a name
   written in an instruction is the same name written in a declaration. *)
identifier     = aksara_word ;
aksara_word    = aksara , { aksara } ;

numeral        = [ "ऋण" ] , ( binary | octal | hexadecimal | decimal ) ;
binary         = "०द्वि"  , bin_digit , { bin_digit } ;
octal          = "०अष्ट" , oct_digit , { oct_digit } ;
hexadecimal    = "०षोड्" , hex_digit , { hex_digit } ;
decimal        = dec_digit , { dec_digit } ;

bin_digit      = "०" | "१" ;
oct_digit      = "०" | "१" | "२" | "३" | "४" | "५" | "६" | "७" ;
dec_digit      = "०" | "१" | "२" | "३" | "४" | "५" | "६" | "७" | "८" | "९" ;
hex_digit      = dec_digit | "अ" | "आ" | "इ" | "ई" | "उ" | "ऊ" ;

comment        = comment_mark , { non_newline } , newline ;

(* --- strings ---------------------------------------------------------- *)

(* ADR-0017. Two earlier decisions are carried here unchanged and only the
 * third belongs to this file:
 *
 *   the DELIMITERS are ADR-0003's. Doc 15 §3.1 spelled them `꣹ … ꣹`; A-033
 *   measured that sign at 0 of 27 Devanagari faces and ADR-0003 replaced it
 *   with `उक्तम् … इति`, "spoken … thus", under Principle P-1. Both words are
 *   attested — `spec/lexicon.src.tsv:77` and `:75`.
 *
 *   the ESCAPE is ADR-0011's. ADR-0003 had named `॰`, and it could never work:
 *   `॰` is the comment mark and the lexer deleted everything after it before a
 *   parser existed. A DOUBLED close takes its place — `इति इति` is the word
 *   itself and the first unpaired `इति` closes — which coins no character, as
 *   `ॱॱ` already does for the colon role.
 *
 *   what is NEW is the STAGE: the lexer reads the string, and reads it BEFORE
 *   it strips the comment. That is why a string may hold a space and a `॰`,
 *   and it is why this production is lexical rather than phrase structure.
 *)
string         = string_open ,
                 { aksara_word | string_close , string_close } ,
                 string_close ;
string_open    = "उक्तम्" | "वर्णाष्टकम्" ;   (* spoken · Devanagari-8 data *)
string_close   = "इति"   ;   (* thus   *)

(* AMENDED 2026-10-06 by ADR-0044 (D2), under the OWNER'S EXEMPTION to this
 * frozen grammar, granted that day "specifically to register `वर्णाष्टकम्` as a
 * valid data-literal string opener alongside `उक्तम्`". It mirrors `उक्तम्`
 * EXACTLY: the same close, the same `इति इति` escape, the same one-line rule.
 * What differs is the VALUE: the letters in Devanagari-8, ONE OCTET PER
 * LETTER, `0x80 + (code point − 0x0900)` over U+0900–U+097F (D1), so the
 * length is the letter count. A code point outside that block — a space, a
 * digit, ASCII, another script — is refused at compile time by
 * `अदेवनागरीवर्णप्रतिषेधः` (owner-named), naming the letter and its column. `उक्तम्` is
 * untouched: every program without the new opener means what it meant. *)

(* A string does not cross a newline: an unclosed one is an error at end of
   line, reported at the opener. Interior whitespace is the text's, taken from
   the source rather than rebuilt; the whitespace separating a delimiter from
   the body belongs to the delimiter, so a string neither begins nor ends with
   a space. Every character of one is still inside doc 15's repertoire, which
   already admits SPACE, TAB and LINE FEED (R-15-1) — THIS PRODUCTION WIDENS NO
   REPERTOIRE, and a Latin letter inside a string is refused exactly as it is
   anywhere else. *)

(* --- embeds ----------------------------------------------------------- *)

(* ADR-0019, task `D-002g`. An EMBED is a derived table brought into a program
 * at ASSEMBLY time. It is the T1 equivalent of Rust's `include_str!`, which is
 * what `crates/sadhana/src/parse.rs:25` uses on THIS spec directory and what
 * `crates/sadhana-t1/src/encode.t1:419` names as the thing T1 lacks.
 *
 * A FILE IS NAMED, NEVER PATHED, and that is the decision rather than a
 * convenience. `spec/mnemonics-riscv64.src.tsv` is Latin letters, `/`, `-` and
 * `.`; the lexer checks the doc 15 repertoire over every word AND over the
 * whole of a string literal, so a path is not writable in this language. The
 * answer is not to widen the repertoire or to exempt this construct from it —
 * it is R-02-1's answer, the one the ISA already gets: a finite reviewed set of
 * NAMES, and no escape hatch. `..` is not refused here; it is unspellable.
 *
 * The name is an ordinary `identifier` and is checked exactly as every other
 * word is. The CONTENT is not source and is not repertoire-checked, for the
 * reason ADR-0003's Foreclosed section already gives about a string's interior:
 * it is data. That is also the whole point of the row — `B-058a` found that a
 * hand-typed second copy of a derived table agrees with the first BY
 * CONSTRUCTION and checks nothing.
 *
 * The delimiters are the ratified `group` pair and nothing is coined. The
 * RESULT is a `string`, so `token` above needs no amendment and every reader of
 * a string literal reads an embed with no second rule — ADR-0017's reason and
 * ADR-0018's, applied a third time.
 *)

embed          = "समावेशः" , "आरभ्य" , identifier , "समाप्तम्" ;

(* T0 IS NOT ON THIS PATH, exactly as it is not on ADR-0017's or ADR-0018's.
 * `spec/grammar-t0.ebnf` is frozen, `crates/sadhana/src/lex.rs` `lex` is
 * untouched, and `समावेशः` remains `.include`, `planned`, in
 * `spec/directives.tsv` — that row is not edited, not reclassified and not
 * deleted by this ADR.
 *
 * SO THE WORD NOW CARRIES TWO SENSES AND THIS FILE SAYS SO RATHER THAN LEAVING
 * THE COLLISION TO LOOK ACCIDENTAL. `.include` splices SOURCE to be parsed —
 * C's `#include`, Rust's `include!`. The production above yields a file's BYTES
 * as a VALUE — Rust's `include_str!`. English double-books that pair in exactly
 * this way and separates them by what follows the word; so does this, and what
 * follows is `आरभ्य … समाप्तम्`.
 *
 * A READER TELLS THEM APART BY THE TIER, AND THAT ALONE IS SUFFICIENT: a
 * directive is T0, read by `spec/grammar-t0.ebnf` and by `lex`; an embed is T1,
 * read by this file and by `lex_t1`. No source is both tiers, so no position is
 * ambiguous. `t0_never_produces_an_embed` in `crates/sadhana/tests/t1_embed.rs`
 * holds it. Two further discriminators back it up: a directive is wrapped in
 * `॥ … ॥` (ADR-0012) and an embed in the group pair (ADR-0003); a directive's
 * argument may be a STRING (ADR-0013, and doc 02 §2.5 writes
 * `॥ समावेशः "तन्त्र/स्मृति" ॥`) where an embed's is a bare identifier and
 * cannot be a path at all.
 *
 * WHAT THIS FORECLOSES, said out loud: if T1 ever wants textual inclusion of
 * its own, it may NOT spell it `समावेशः`, because in T1 that word is the embed.
 * That cost is accepted — no row asks for it, and even the T0 form is
 * `planned`. *)

(* --- terminals, all from ADR-0003 ------------------------------------- *)

danda          = "।"  ;   (* U+0964  statement end                       *)
double_danda   = "॥"  ;   (* U+0965  declaration end                     *)
comment_mark   = "॰"  ;   (* U+0970  comment to end of line              *)
member_mark    = "ॱ"  ;   (* U+0971  member access                       *)
annotation     = "ॱॱ" ;   (* U+0971 ×2  the colon role                   *)
separator      = "ऽ"  ;   (* U+093D  argument separator                  *)

(* ===================================================================== *)
(* MODULES — ADR-0026, task `D-002l`. Added 2026-08-29.                   *)
(* FROZEN on the same terms as everything above.                          *)
(* ===================================================================== *)

(* --- what was missing, and what was not ------------------------------- *)

(* The IMPORT half was frozen already: `आयातः` is in the `keyword` production
 * above and 23 `आयातः` statements stand in 14 `.t1` sources. What was
 * missing was the DECLARING half — the thing being imported had no production,
 * so `आयातः` named nothing that this grammar could define.
 *
 * `मण्डलम्` is not coined here. `spec/lexicon.src.tsv:271` already gives it as
 * `module`, `keyword`, `attested`; 19 of the 20 `.t1` sources in the tree
 * declare with `मण्डलम् <name>`; and `crates/sadhana/src/t1/drishya.rs` reads
 * the word for T2. Adding it here ADOPTS a word that the corpus, the lexicon and
 * one front end were all already using, and closes the gap the grammar had:
 * a construct asserted by nearly every source file and defined nowhere.
 *
 * It cannot collide with a name. `no_t1_source_binds_a_frozen_keyword_as_a_name`
 * flags a keyword only where it FOLLOWS a declaring word or PRECEDES `ॱॱ`, and
 * `मण्डलम्` LEADS its declaration in every one of the 19.
 *)

module_decl    = "मण्डलम्" , identifier , double_danda ;
import         = "आयातः" , identifier , danda ;

(* --- reaching into another module ------------------------------------- *)

(* A qualified name is `member_mark`, ADR-0003's ratified sign, applied to a
 * module rather than to a value. NO SIGN IS COINED and no new shape is
 * introduced, which is why this is one production and not a section.
 *)

qualified_name = identifier , member_mark , identifier ;

(* TWO THINGS ABOUT THIS PRODUCTION ARE LOAD-BEARING, AND BOTH WERE MEASURED
 * IN THE CORPUS RATHER THAN ASSUMED.
 *
 * ONE — IT HAS TWO SPELLINGS, AND THE LEXER TELLS THEM APART, NOT THE PARSER.
 * `crates/sadhana/src/lex.rs` `split_trailing_punct` peels `ॱॱ । ॥ ऽ` off the
 * end of a word but NEVER the single `ॱ`, and `punctuation` maps a standalone
 * `ॱ` to `Kind::MemberMark`. So the UNSPACED form `सङ्केतनॱस्थापनम्` arrives as
 * ONE token whose text holds the mark, and the SPACED form `सङ्केतन ॱ स्थापनम्`
 * as THREE. The corpus writes both — 228 unspaced against 34 spaced, counted by
 * `the_corpus_declares_modules_and_reaches_across_them` — so a reader that
 * knows only the unspaced form misses 34 of 262 references and one that knows
 * only the spaced form misses 228 of them.
 *
 * `ॱॱ` IS NOT `ॱ`, AND A READER MUST NOT TAKE THE FIRST HALF OF ONE FOR THE
 * OTHER. `crates/sadhana-t1/src/samyojana.t1:74` is `सारणी ॱॱ अङ्कः अन्तः
 * कोशॱसंज्ञा` — the FIELD `सारणी` and the annotation mark — and `सारणी` is also
 * a module, declared at `crates/textapp/src/text/tables.t1:1`. A reader that
 * splits `ॱॱ` reads that field as a qualified name into a module the file never
 * imports, and invents a fourth unimported module out of a colon.
 *
 * A READER OVER TOKENS GETS THIS FOR FREE AND A READER OVER WORDS DOES NOT.
 * The lexer already ratified the distinction: `ॱॱ` is `Kind::LabelMark`, `ॱ` is
 * `Kind::MemberMark`, two kinds, one peeled before the other. That is why
 * `mandala` reads tokens; widening its spaced case to accept a label mark was
 * tried, and `exactly_three_modules_are_reached_without_being_imported` went
 * red with the fourth module in it.
 *
 * TWO — THE SHAPE DOES NOT SAY WHICH IT IS. `पदम् ॱ पाठ` is a FIELD of a local
 * and `सङ्केतन ॱ पङ्क्तिसीमा` is a routine of a module, and the two are the same
 * three tokens. The head is a MODULE only when it names one, so this is settled
 * at RESOLUTION against the set of declared modules and cannot be settled by the
 * grammar. `crates/sadhana/src/t1/mandala.rs` is that resolution and
 * `crates/sadhana-t1/tests/t1_modules.rs` holds it against the corpus.
 *)

(* WHAT THIS DOES NOT SETTLE, said out loud rather than left to be found:
 *
 *   - VISIBILITY. `सार्वजनिक` is in the frozen keyword list and 19 sources write
 *     it, but nothing yet says a qualified name may reach only a `सार्वजनिक`
 *     member. The declaration half had to exist before that rule could bite.
 *   - WHERE A MODULE'S SOURCE LIVES. A name is resolved against the modules
 *     DECLARED in the compilation set; no search path, no file-name convention
 *     and no directory rule is frozen here. `कओश` and `सारणी` show why the
 *     question is real — `crates/sadhana-t1/src/kosha.t1` declares `कओश` while
 *     two sources import `कोश`, and no rule yet says whether that is one module
 *     or two.
 *   - SUBMODULES. `crates/sadhana-t1/src/artha.t1:28` says outright that three
 *     Rust modules are one `मण्डलम्` here "because T1 has no submodules". This
 *     production keeps it that way: a module name is one `identifier`.
 *)

(* ===================================================================== *)
(* PHRASE STRUCTURE — grouping, blocks, indexing. Task B-079b.            *)
(* Added 2026-08-25. FROZEN on the same terms as the lexical layer above:  *)
(* changing a production below is an ADR, not an edit.                    *)
(* ===================================================================== *)

(* --- why this could be settled without the readability study ---------- *)

(* The section this replaces said the three constructs waited on `A-063`,
 * because the alternatives — words, reusing `॥`, or an off-side rule —
 * "differ in exactly the property A-063 measures". The owner ruled on
 * 2026-08-25 that A-063's evidence is NOT a precondition for phrase
 * structure. That ruling alone would still leave a choice to make.
 *
 * IT DOES NOT, BECAUSE THE CHOICE WAS ALREADY MADE AND RATIFIED. The old text
 * asserted "there is no ratified sign for any of them", which was true of
 * SIGNS and missed that ADR-0003 had already replaced these exact three roles
 * with WORDS, in its "Replaced — paired keywords" table, under Principle P-1
 * (prefer words to signs) and on the same A-033 font measurement:
 *
 *     group   `꣼ ꣸`  ->  आरभ्य   … समाप्तम्   (begun … completed)
 *     block   `꣺ ꣻ`  ->  आदि     … इति        (beginning … thus)
 *     index   `ᳵ ᳶ`  ->  अङ्कः    … अन्तः      (index … end)
 *     string  `꣹ ꣹`  ->  उक्तम्   … इति        (spoken … thus)
 *
 * ADR-0003 is `accepted`, so adopting its own table is not a guess and not a
 * new decision — it is applying a ratified one to the tier that needs it. What
 * A-063 would still inform is whether operator-words are too verbose, which is
 * a different question about different tokens, and is why OPERATORS ARE NOT
 * FROZEN HERE (see below).
 *)

(* --- the three constructs --------------------------------------------- *)

group          = "आरभ्य" , expression , "समाप्तम्" ;
block          = "आदि" , { statement } , "इति" ;
index          = "अङ्कः" , expression , "अन्तः" ;

(* --- the type constructors that were waiting on them ------------------ *)

(* Deferred at line 97 above because each takes an operand, and the slice
   spelling needed `ᳵ ᳶ`. With the index marks ratified as words, the slice is
   the EMPTY index pair before a type, which is the same shape doc 02 §3.2
   sketched as `[]T`. *)
slice_type     = "अङ्कः" , "अन्तः" , type ;
pointer_type   = "स्थानम्" , type ;
optional_type  = "सम्भाव्य" , type ;
error_union    = "दोषयुक्त" , type ;

(* ADDED 2026-08-29 by ADR-0026 (`D-002k`), OWNER-RATIFIED: a FIXED-CAPACITY
 * ARRAY. It amends no production above; `slice_type` is untouched and is still
 * exactly what it was.
 *
 * IT COINS NO WORD, and that is the reason for this spelling rather than a new
 * keyword. `keyword` is untouched, `type_name` is untouched, and the terminals
 * below are `अङ्कः` and `अन्तः`, both of which this file already carries as the
 * `index` pair ADR-0003 ratified. The bound is a `numeral`, which is a token
 * this file already defines and which ADR-0023 already spells in Devanagari
 * digits. So `every_terminal_survives_orthographic_closure` and
 * `no_terminal_is_a_sign_that_renders_in_no_face` in
 * `crates/sanskrit-text/tests/grammar_t1.rs` have nothing new to weigh — the
 * third application of ADR-0017's reason, after ADR-0018's and ADR-0019's.
 *
 * A SLICE IS THIS TYPE WITH THE BOUND LEFT OUT. Doc 02 §3.2 sketched `[]T` and
 * `[N]T` as the same brackets differing by what is inside them; the index marks
 * are this language's brackets, so they differ here by the same thing. No
 * source that parsed before parses differently: the numeral is the only
 * discriminator and no slice ever had one.
 *
 * THE BOUND IS COMPILE-TIME AND THAT IS THE POINT. It is a `numeral` TOKEN, not
 * an `expression`, so it can be read with operators still deferred (see "what
 * is STILL not frozen") and so no capacity can depend on a runtime value. T1
 * has no allocator and this production does not ask for one — it is the
 * capacity `ast.t1:65` and `parse.t1:36` need in order to write down the
 * arenas they already index into, and it is deliberately NOT a growable
 * collection. `artha.t1:444` wants growth and is not answered here.
 *)

array_type     = "अङ्कः" , numeral , "अन्तः" , type ;

(* --- `इति` CLOSES TWO THINGS, AND THAT IS RESOLVED LEXICALLY ---------- *)

(* ADR-0003 gives `इति` as both the block close and the string close. That is
 * not an ambiguity the parser has to resolve, because a string is a LEXICAL
 * construct: `उक्तम्` opens it and the first unpaired `इति` closes it, a
 * DOUBLED `इति` standing for the word itself (ADR-0011). The lexer therefore
 * consumes the whole string, including its `इति`, before the parser sees a
 * token — exactly as a quote-delimited string works in an ASCII language — and
 * a `इति` inside a string is never offered as a candidate block close.
 *
 * THE CONSEQUENCE THIS NOTE USED TO RECORD IS DISCHARGED. It read: "this only
 * holds if the lexer knows about strings, and the FROZEN `token` production
 * above does not list one … Until it is, a T1 front end (B-080) cannot lex a
 * string literal." `token` now lists `string`, `crates/sadhana/src/lex.rs`
 * `lex_t1` reads it, and `F-004f2` is the row that did it under ADR-0017.
 *
 * ONE THING THIS NOTE GOT WRONG IS CORRECTED RATHER THAN QUIETLY DROPPED: it
 * said `॰` escapes the following akṣara, so a literal `इति` was `॰इति`.
 * ADR-0011 had already superseded that clause of ADR-0003 — `॰` is the comment
 * mark and an escape written with it was deleted before any parser could see
 * it, unreachable by construction rather than merely unimplemented. The escape
 * is the doubled close, and `॰` inside a string is now ordinary text.
 *
 * T0 IS NOT ON THIS PATH. `spec/grammar-t0.ebnf` still spells `string` as a
 * phrase and `crates/sadhana/src/parse.rs` still reassembles it from words, so
 * ADR-0011's "`॰` cannot appear inside a string" still stands THERE. The two
 * tiers agree on the spelling of a string and differ on which stage reads it;
 * unifying them is a successor row, and ADR-0017 says so out loud rather than
 * leaving the divergence to be found.
 *)

(* ===================================================================== *)
(* EXPRESSIONS AND OPERATORS — ADR-0026, task `D-002h`.                   *)
(* Added 2026-08-30. FROZEN on the same terms as everything above it:      *)
(* changing a production below is an ADR, not an edit.                    *)
(* ===================================================================== *)

(* --- why this could be settled, after standing open since v0.1 -------- *)

(* The note this replaces said operators waited on `A-063`'s readability
 * study, because doc 02 §3.2's `= + * →` is a self-declared ASCII sketch and
 * doc 15 §3.4-3.5 — where the normative spelling was deferred to — was never
 * written. That reasoning is retired for two reasons, not one.
 *
 * `A-063` is `dropped` and ADR-0003 foreclosed sign operators permanently, so
 * the study cannot arrive and would not decide this if it did.
 *
 * AND THE SPELLINGS WERE NEVER ACTUALLY MISSING. The 9,721-line `.t1` corpus
 * under `crates/sadhana-t1/src/` writes them, and has for as long as it has
 * existed: `योगः` 393 times, `भवति` 1,788, `समम्` 340. The owner ruled on
 * 2026-08-29 that WHERE THREE DESCRIPTIONS DISAGREE — `spec/lexicon.tsv`'s
 * 24 `category=operator` rows, this corpus, and
 * `crates/tree-sitter-t1/grammar.js` — THE CORPUS IS CANONICAL. So freezing
 * here is transcription of a ratified source, which is what this file does
 * everywhere else, and not the invention it refused to make.
 *
 * ADR-0026 records the counts, the two ungrouped corpus constructions that
 * fix the precedence, and the four operators the lexicon proposes that the
 * corpus does NOT have (modulo, `<=`/`>=`, logical and/or/not, bitwise not).
 * None of those four is added. *)

(* --- the operators ---------------------------------------------------- *)

(* The thirteen binary operators are exactly `ast.t1`'s thirteen
   `*द्विकर्मभेद` kind constants (`ast.t1:15`-`:27`), and the guard test asserts
   that correspondence in BOTH directions so neither set can grow alone. Twelve
   are attested in source; `विषम` (xor) is named by the AST and written by no
   source, and is frozen on the AST's authority — ADR-0026 says so out loud. *)

mul_op         = "गुणनम्"  | "विभाजनम्" | "शेषः" ;
add_op         = "योगः"   | "वियोगः" ;
shift_op       = "वामसृ"  | "दक्षिणसृ" ;
and_op         = "युक्" ;
xor_op         = "विषम" ;
or_op          = "विकल्प" ;
compare_op     = "समम्" | "असमम्" | "न्यूनम्" | "अधिकम्" | "बृहत्समम्" ;

(* `ऋण` is the ONLY unary operator. It is also the numeral's sign prefix
   above, and the two are separated in the LEXER by whether a digit follows it
   with no space — `ऋण१` is one token and `ऋण सीमा` is two. ADR-0026 §`ऋण` has
   two roles. *)
unary_op       = "ऋण" ;

(* THE LEXICON HAS BEEN BROUGHT TO THESE WORDS — ADR-0028. `spec/lexicon.tsv`
   proposed `अधि`/`ऊन`/`गुण`/`भाग`/`विषमम्` from doc 15 for five of the senses
   above; no source writes any of them, so they are retired in favour of
   `योगः`/`वियोगः`/`गुणनम्`/`विभाजनम्`/`असमम्`. The first three are T0 mnemonic
   rows that now serve both tiers rather than new operator rows. Eight lexicon
   operators SURVIVE unattested — `शेष`, `च`, `वा`, `न`, `व्यत्यय`, `न्यूनसमम्`,
   `अधिकसमम्`, `पूर्णक्रमेण` — because this grammar has no production for
   their senses at all, so nothing here contradicts them. (2026-10-06, owner
   ruling on W-381 stage 3 (f): a ninth, the wrapping-plus word, was RETIRED
   from the lexicon when wrapping became the default arithmetic, and its
   mention here is dropped. Comment only; no production changed.) *)

(* --- the precedence ladder -------------------------------------------- *)

(* Rust's ladder, adopted whole because the corpus is a line-by-line port of
   Rust and two ungrouped constructions in it agree: `१ वामसृ विस्तारः वियोगः १`
   (`encode.t1:244`) is `encode.rs:153`'s `1i64 << (width - 1)`, so ADDITIVE
   BINDS TIGHTER THAN SHIFT; and `कोशः ॱ दैर्घ्य न्यूनम् सूचकाङ्क योगः १`
   (`artha.t1:367`) is `len < index + 1`, so ADDITIVE BINDS TIGHTER THAN
   COMPARISON. Each level is left-associative except comparison, which is
   NON-ASSOCIATIVE: `अ समम् आ समम् इ` has no reading anyone means, and the
   corpus never writes one. *)

expression     = compare_expr ;

compare_expr   = or_expr , [ compare_op , or_expr ] ;
or_expr        = xor_expr    , { or_op    , xor_expr } ;
xor_expr       = and_expr    , { xor_op   , and_expr } ;
and_expr       = shift_expr  , { and_op   , shift_expr } ;
shift_expr     = add_expr    , { shift_op , add_expr } ;
add_expr       = mul_expr    , { add_op   , mul_expr } ;
mul_expr       = unary_expr  , { mul_op   , unary_expr } ;
unary_expr     = { unary_op } , call_expr ;

(* Member access and indexing are POSTFIX and bind tighter than any operator:
   `युग्मम् ॱ मूलांश युक् १` is `(pair.lo) & 1`, not `pair.(lo & 1)`. `अङ्कः …
   अन्तः` is the frozen `index` production above, reused here rather than
   respelled. *)
postfix_expr   = primary , { member_mark , identifier | slice | index } ;

primary        = identifier | numeral | string | group ;

(* THE SLICE — added 2026-09-04 by W-228, and it is the INTERPRETER'S rule
   written down, not a new one: `nirvahana.rs`'s `postfix` reads `अङ्कः … अन्तः`
   and then, "if the next token can open an atom", takes a second postfix
   expression as the LIMIT and builds a slice; otherwise the index closes.
   The corpus has 20 such sites (W-208) — `पाठ्यम् अङ्कः आरम्भः अन्तः सीमा` —
   and the T1 parser, having no production for them, read each as a call
   applied to an index. Now both readers build one tree.

   THE RULE THAT MAKES IT DECIDABLE, measured against every `अङ्कः … अन्तः` in
   the corpus and stated on the interpreter's side first: an `index` followed
   by anything that can begin a `primary` is a `slice`, NEVER an index that
   happens to precede a further call argument. The corpus never writes the
   latter. The limit is a `postfix_expr` — a name, a member chain, an index —
   and not an `expression`, because an operator after it must still bind the
   whole slice as its left operand: `क अङ्कः १ अन्तः २ ॱ दैर्घ्य` is
   `(क[1..2.length])`, `… अन्तः २ योगः ३` is `(क[1..2]) + 3`.

   REFUSED: `क अङ्कः अन्तः २` — an index with no lower bound is refused where
   the empty `अङ्कः अन्तः` would be read (`W-228`'s refused case in
   `t1_paradigm_calls.rs`); `अङ्कः अन्तः <type>` is the `slice_type`, a
   different production in a different position. *)
slice          = index , postfix_expr ;

(* THE SPACED QUALIFIER, `सङ्केतन ॱ स्थापनम्`, READS AS `qualified_name` AND
   BUILDS THE SAME TREE AS `सङ्केतनॱस्थापनम्` — stated 2026-09-04 by W-228,
   applying ADR-0027 ("the head is a module only when it names one, so the
   distinction is settled at RESOLUTION against the set of declared modules")
   to the parser's TREE rather than only to its resolver. 127 call sites
   (W-208) write the qualifier with spaces; the T1 parser read them as a
   member reached on the module's name, the interpreter as a qualified call,
   and the two met the same routine by two trees. Rule X1 — a span is named
   by its pair — applied to the member mark: one construct, one tree. So
   `व्याकर` folds `identifier , member_mark , identifier` into one `identifier`
   node whenever the head names a module this file IMPORTS (`आयातः`), and
   the resolver reaches that node through the same arm as the unspaced
   spelling. A head that is not an imported module stays a member access —
   which is what it is. The fold is decided by the import list alone, so it
   is sound only while no local or parameter is named like an imported
   module; the corpus has none, and `t1_paradigm_calls.rs` pins that as the
   precondition. *)

(* --- the call, which `statement` had to answer first ------------------ *)

(* ADDED 2026-08-30 by ADR-0027 (`D-002h`). ADR-0026 left this out and said
   why: T1 spells a call as a bare juxtaposition, so *where the argument list
   ENDS* was open, and the note above ruled that "that decision belongs with
   `statement`". `statement` is below, and the answer fell out of writing it.

   IT IS NOT OPTIONAL AND IT IS NOT SMALL. Of the corpus's 1,788 `भवति`
   right-hand sides, 299 contain a juxtaposition — `चरः विस्तारः ॱॱ अ६४ भवति
   अवकाशविस्तारः अवकाशः ।` (`encode.t1:213`) — and a further 200 sites write
   the BRACKETED form. Without `call_expr` the frozen `expression` cannot read
   any of them, so `statement` would have been a production that parses no
   assignment the corpus actually writes.

   WHERE THE JUXTAPOSED LIST ENDS, measured rather than chosen: at the first
   token that cannot begin a `primary`. Every terminator in the corpus is one
   of those — `।` (`अष्टकान्वेषणम् पाठ्यम् स्थितिः सीमा ९ ।`), an operator
   (`यदि चिह्नितावकाशः अवकाशः ॱ भेद समम् असत्यम् आदि`, where the list stops at
   `समम्`), `समाप्तम्`, `अन्तः`, `ऽ` and `आदि`. So the rule is MAXIMAL MUNCH
   and nothing else, and it needs no delimiter to be decidable.

   THE LIST IS FLAT, SAID OUT LOUD: `घ क ख` is `घ(क, ख)` and never `घ(क)(ख)`.
   The corpus has no value that is itself callable, so the two readings are not
   distinguishable from it; the flat one is frozen because it is the one every
   site means. A nested call is written with the bracket — `मूल्याङ्कनम् ०`
   appears as an argument INSIDE `आज्ञायोजनम् आरभ्य … समाप्तम्` at
   `ir.t1:338`.

   THE BRACKETED FORM REQUIRES TWO ARGUMENTS, and that is what removes the
   ambiguity with `group` rather than a lookahead. `घ आरभ्य क समाप्तम्` is
   already a juxtaposed call over a `group`, and the two readings AGREE, so
   only `n >= 2` — which needs `ऽ` and which `group` cannot be — is given to
   the bracket. 200 sites write it; all 200 carry at least one `ऽ`. *)

call_expr      = postfix_expr , [ argument_list ] ;

argument_list  = "आरभ्य" , expression , separator , expression ,
                             { separator , expression } , "समाप्तम्"
               | postfix_expr , { postfix_expr } ;

(* --- what this deliberately leaves out -------------------------------- *)

(* `भवति` (1,788 occurrences) IS NOT AN EXPRESSION OPERATOR. Every one of its
 * occurrences is statement-initial or declarative — `चरः क्ष ॱॱ ट भवति ङ ।`,
 * `क्ष भवति ङ ।`, `क्ष ॱ फ भवति ङ ।` — and none is inside an operand. It is the
 * assignment STATEMENT's verb, so it belongs to `statement`, which is still
 * deferred. Putting it on the ladder as a right-associative bottom level would
 * describe a language the corpus does not write.
 *
 * `call` WAS likewise absent, and for a sharper reason: T1 spells a call as a
 * bare juxtaposition — `परिसरान्वेषणम् अन्तिमः नाम` — with no delimiter of any
 * kind, so `primary` above could not be extended to it without deciding how a
 * call's argument list ends. That decision was said to belong with
 * `statement`, and it did: ADR-0027 answers it in `call_expr` below, where
 * the terminator turns out to be measurable rather than choosable. This
 * paragraph is kept in the past tense rather than deleted, because a reader
 * who remembers `call` as open should find out here that it is not. *)


