   THE TWO KEYWORD FIGURES ARE UNMOVED at 32 and 15, which is the half worth
   saying: this row added no alternative to the frozen keyword production and
   wrote none of them as a name. It nearly did — `गणना` was bound twice as a
   counter and `no_t1_source_binds_a_frozen_keyword_as_a_name` refused it.

   SUPERSEDED 2026-09-05 (`W-266`), and the move is FIVE STATEMENTS in one
   routine. `सङ्कोचः` (`encode.t1:4857`) saved this module's one-slot error
   state by BINDING a second name to it, which shares; the save is now five
   field copies onto a fresh record, so five assignments appeared and no
   binding did. Only the two keys that can move that way moved:
     was  assignments            1920   ->  1925
     was  भवति                   4934   ->  4939
   `bindings`, `चरः` and `public-bindings` are unchanged, which is the check
   on the claim: a field write is an assignment and never a binding.

   SUPERSEDED 2026-09-05, taken from `w268-census.rs`'s own failing assertion
   when `W-248` landed a text-to-type reader into `artha.t1` and the corpus
   grew under the guard. Kept with its date rather than overwritten, because a
   file that shows its own corrections tells the next reader when each figure
   was last true:
     was  assignments            1905   ->  1920
     was  bindings               3000   ->  3014
     was  public-bindings        511    ->  516
     was  चरः                    3000   ->  3014
     was  प्रत्यागमनम्           1764   ->  1773
     was  भवति                   4905   ->  4934
     was  यदि                    1940   ->  1952
     was  यावत्                  300    ->  301
     was  public-bindings        516    ->  517   (2026-09-05, the trunk's red-main
                                                  fix: `प्रकारपाठविवराष्टकम्` hoisted
                                                  out of a routine body to module
                                                  scope, so one more binding is
                                                  `सार्वजनिक`)
   `keyword-alternatives` and `keyword-written` did not move: the reader added
   routines, not keywords. THE GUARD CAUGHT THIS ON THE MERGE, which is what it
   was built for one commit earlier.


   SUPERSEDED 2026-08-30 (ADR-0027, `D-002h`), KEPT WITH ITS DATE: `चरः`
   1,039, `भवति` 1,788, `यदि` 670, `अन्यथा` 29, `यावत्` 120, `प्रत्यागमनम्`
   779; 1,039 bindings and 749 assignments. Every one is roughly a THIRD of
   today's, and THE SHAPE OF THE CLAIM SURVIVED ALL OF IT — the six forms are
   still the six, the two sets still sum to the whole. That is why the old
   figures are kept rather than overwritten: they show that the sentence was
   right and only its numbers were dead, which is the harder thing for a
   reader to learn from a file that shows only its current answer. The defect
   was never arithmetic. It was that a figure sat in a comment for six days
   with NOTHING RE-MEASURING IT while the corpus tripled around it, and the
   sentences resting on it went on being read as current.

   AND THE SPELLINGS FOR THE REST ALREADY EXIST HERE AND ARE UNUSED, which is
   the finding worth keeping. `keyword` above has 32 alternatives — doc 02
   §3.2's v0.1 set, frozen by `B-079a`, plus `मण्डलम्` (ADR-0027) and the two
   layout words (ADR-0018) — and the corpus writes FIFTEEN of them.
   SUPERSEDED 2026-08-30: "`keyword`'s 24 words … the corpus writes ELEVEN".
   The 24 never matched the production as printed above under any of its
   amendments; it is the same defect as the counts, one level up, and it is
   why the guard reads the alternative list OUT OF THIS FILE rather than
   keeping a copy of its own.

   The five that would each be a statement form are still written ZERO times:
   `प्रत्येकम्` (for), `भङ्गः` (break), `अनुवर्तनम्` (continue), `विकल्पना`
   (switch) and `रक्षा` (defer). The guard checks those five BY NAME rather
   than counting how many words are unwritten, because one of them appearing
   while another fell out of use would leave such a count untouched. So T1 as
   written has ONE loop form and NO loop-exit statement, and this file does not
   invent the productions — but nothing will need inventing when the corpus
   grows one, because the word is already ratified.

   THE OTHER TWELVE UNWRITTEN WORDS, listed rather than summarised because the
   2026-08-30 note said "the other three" and then named seven: declaration-tier
   `प्रकारः`, `संघः`, `ध्रुवः`; tooling `प्रयत्नः`, `दोषः`, `समयकृत`,
   `परीक्षा`; the value words `रिक्तः`, `स्वयम्`, `असुरक्षितम्`; and
   ADR-0018's two layout words `यतिः` and `विवरम्`, which the corpus writes
   nowhere at all — ratified 2026-08-28 and unspelled by any source since. *)

statement      = binding
               | assignment
               | conditional
               | loop
               | return_statement
               | expression_statement ;

(* A BINDING ALWAYS CARRIES ITS TYPE — there is no inference, and that is a
   count and not an impression. The `भवति` sites partition EXACTLY into the
   `bindings` and `assignments` figures above: every binding is led by `चरः`,
   with or without `सार्वजनिक`, and every one of them has a `ॱॱ type` before
   the verb; the assignments are the remainder and not one of them has a `ॱॱ`.
   The two sets sum to the whole `भवति` count, so `[ ॱॱ type ]` would describe
   a form the corpus never writes. All three of those — the sum, the presence
   in every binding, the absence in every assignment — are asserted by the
   guard, not just the totals.

   THE DIGITS ARE DELIBERATELY NOT REPEATED IN THIS PARAGRAPH. Until
   2026-09-05 they were, and a figure written in two places dies in one of them
   first; the census above is the single site and this paragraph names its
   keys.

   `सार्वजनिक` IS PART OF THE BINDING, AND A CENSUS THAT DROPS IT SPLITS THE
   PARTITION WRONG — worth stating because one already did. `public-bindings`
   of the bindings above are `सार्वजनिक चरः`, so a walk that tests only a
   statement's FIRST token for `चरः` moves every one of them to the assignment
   side. `crates/sadhana-t1/tests/w250-shares.rs` does exactly that, which is
   why its split of this same corpus differs from the one above by exactly that
   number while its `भवति` total agrees to the site. The structural claim is
   what decides between the two splits rather than an opinion about the
   production: under the other one, `public-bindings` "assignments" carry a
   `ॱॱ`, and the sentence above would be false. *)
binding        = [ "सार्वजनिक" ] , "चरः" , identifier , annotation , type ,
                 "भवति" , expression , danda ;

(* --- scope: what a module's names are, and what a local is ------------ *)

(* STATED 2026-09-04 (W-222), HERE AND NOT BESIDE `module_decl`, because the
   precedence ladder above is cited by line number (673-690) from research/22,
   doc 15 and the W-217 audit, and a note placed before it would move them.
   The rule concerns `module_decl` and `binding` equally; it sits by the
   second. The language has behaved this way since the resolver was ported
   and no document said so; `W-209` measured it — 51 top-level uses precede
   their declaration in the same module, 0 local uses precede their `चरः` —
   and the rule is written here so the measurement has something to be
   measured AGAINST.

   A MODULE'S TOP-LEVEL NAMES ARE MODULE-SCOPED AND ORDER-FREE. Every routine,
   type, machine, top-level `binding` and import declared in a module is
   visible throughout the module, before and after the line that declares it.
   `crates/sadhana-t1/src/artha.t1`'s `कार्यक्रमनिर्णयः` keeps this in two
   passes — every top-level name is DECLARED before any body is RESOLVED —
   and its margin says why: "resolving bodies as they are met would refuse a
   forward reference that the language permits". The corpus relies on it 51
   times (`artha.t1` 25, `encode.t1` 9, `parse.t1` 7, `vakyavibhaga.t1` 4,
   `sanskrit_text.t1` 3, `utsarjana.t1` 2, `samyojana.t1` 1), pinned per
   file as a CEILING in `crates/sadhana-t1/tests/t1_paradigm_names.rs` —
   52 since 2026-09-04, when W-204's call arm gave `ir.t1` its first
   (`ir.t1:696 आह्वानादानरचना`) and the ratchet caught it on the next merge.

   AND THE EXTENT IT RESOLVES IS ONE ITS CALLER PASSES. W-223 part 2,
   2026-09-04. "Throughout the module" needs the module's own bounds, and
   `कार्यक्रमनिर्णयः` takes them as the `कार्यक्रमः` it already declared: the
   count `व्याकर ॱ कार्यक्रमपठनम्` answers, which is that program's last
   declaration index, so the walk is `१ .. कार्यक्रमः`. It is not the
   declaration arena's length, and the difference is a rule and not an
   optimisation: `कार्यक्रमपठनम्` resets `घोषणासूचकाङ्क` on entry but nothing
   truncates `घोषणाकोश`, so a program parsed after a longer one shares an
   arena still holding the longer one's tail. Walking the arena declared
   THOSE names into this program's scope — a name a module never wrote,
   visible throughout it. One interpreter per program hid it; the shared
   declaration store is what ends that, since it is filled by parsing every
   source into one. An extent that cannot be walked — negative, or past the
   arena's own length — is REFUSED by name and never clamped, because a
   clamped extent resolves some other program than the one asked for and
   reports success for it. An extent of ० is not a refusal: it is what
   `कार्यक्रमपठनम्` answers for a program with no declarations, and walking
   none of them is the right answer to it. `कार्यक्रमप्रकारपरीक्षा` takes the
   extent the same way, for the same reason.

   A LOCAL IS VISIBLE FROM ITS `चरः` TO THE END OF ITS BLOCK, AND NOT BEFORE.
   A `binding` inside a `block` declares its name for the statements that
   FOLLOW it in that block and in blocks nested there. A use of the name
   before the `चरः` — in an earlier statement, or in the `चरः`'s own
   `expression` — is not that local: it resolves outward if an enclosing
   scope binds the name (a parameter, a module-level name; a shadow-to-come),
   and is REFUSED as undeclared otherwise. `वाक्यनिर्णयः`'s block arm walks a
   block's statements in written order for exactly this reason, and its
   margin records the alternative and rejects it: declaring every `चरः` up
   front "accepts a use BEFORE its declaration, which is not
   order-sensitivity but its absence".
   `a_block_resolves_its_statements_in_written_order` (`t1_execution.rs`)
   holds the refusal, and `t1_paradigm_names.rs`'s ratchet holds the corpus
   at ZERO such uses, with the refused case run first.

   Read against research/22 §7: a module's names are the vowels of one run —
   they sound alone and in any order — and a local is a marker inside its
   span: it means something only from where it is written to where the span
   closes. That is this rule, arrived at from the other side. *)

(* The assignable places are exactly the three the corpus assigns to: a bare
   name (352), a member (151 + 2), and an index (242 + 2). That is
   `postfix_expr` with `primary` narrowed to `identifier` — the corpus never
   assigns to a numeral, a string or a group, and `place` says so rather than
   reusing `postfix_expr` and leaving the impossible cases to a later pass. *)
place          = identifier , { member_mark , identifier | index } ;
assignment     = place , "भवति" , expression , danda ;

(* `अन्यथा` NEVER TAKES A CONDITION. All 29 occurrences are the exact three
   tokens `इति अन्यथा आदि`, so there is no `else if` chain in this language;
   a nested `यदि` inside the `अन्यथा` block is how the corpus writes one. *)
conditional    = "यदि" , expression , block , [ "अन्यथा" , block ] ;

(* One loop form. `यावत्` is a while, always over a `block`. *)
loop           = "यावत्" , expression , block ;

(* `प्रत्यागमनम्` ALWAYS carries a value: 779 of 779 are followed by an
   expression and NONE by a bare `।`. A void return is not written here, so
   the expression is required rather than optional. *)
return_statement = "प्रत्यागमनम्" , expression , danda ;

(* A bare expression is a statement only when it is a CALL — 18 sites, all of
   them one of `call_expr`'s two shapes. An identifier or a numeral alone is
   never a statement in the corpus, and admitting `expression` here would make
   `क्ष ।` legal for no reason anyone wrote down. *)
expression_statement = postfix_expr , argument_list , danda ;

(* --- the routine ------------------------------------------------------ *)

(* ADDED 2026-09-04 by W-226. THIS FILE HAD NO PRODUCTION FOR A ROUTINE AT ALL:
   `वृत्तिः` sat in `keyword` since B-079a and nothing said what follows it.
   What follows it is MEASURED, not chosen, from the 463 routines of the corpus.

   `ददाति type` IS MANDATORY, AND THERE IS NO VOID. 463 of 463 routine headers
   carry `ददाति` and a type; the class "routine without `ददाति`" does not
   exist in the corpus, and could not: `वृत्तिपठनम्`'s parameter loop ended
   only at `ददाति`, so a header without it was not a procedure but a RUNAWAY
   PARSE that swallowed the body silently (its own margin records the day it
   was found). The corpus writes no void spelling either — no `ददाति शून्यम्`,
   no `निष्फलम्`: a routine that exists for effect declares `ददाति अ६४` and
   its callers bind the result (`nidana.t1`'s header says so). `return_statement`
   above agrees: 779 of 779 returns carry a value. research/23 §6.5 asked
   whether procedures exist by omission; they do not, and the grammar says so.

   THE PARSER REFUSES a header without `ददाति` at the routine's NAME — the
   error record carries the name token's line and column and the reason
   `वृत्तेः ददाति अपेक्षितम्` — and reads on, so one missing word is one
   error and not a cascade. `t1_paradigm_calls.rs` holds the refused case.

   PARAMETERS: `आदाय` opens the list, each parameter is `name ॱॱ type`, and the
   separator between two parameters is OPTIONAL — 132 headers with two or
   more parameters write `ऽ` between them and 133 do not (`आदाय वामः ॱॱ
   अर्थप्रकार दक्षिणः ॱॱ अर्थप्रकार`), so `[ separator ]` is the measurement
   and not a leniency. A parameter's type ends where the next `identifier ,
   annotation` or `ददाति` begins; `type` is self-delimiting in every shape
   the corpus writes. *)
routine        = [ "सार्वजनिक" ] , "वृत्तिः" , identifier ,
                 [ "आदाय" , parameter , { [ separator ] , parameter } ] ,
                 "ददाति" , type , block ;
parameter      = identifier , annotation , type ;

(* --- the type --------------------------------------------------------- *)

(* This one was almost written: `type_name` and the four constructors were
   already above, needing only to be gathered. Two things were NOT already
   there, and both are measurements.

   FIRST, A TYPE CAN BE QUALIFIED. 229 tokens in the corpus carry a `ॱ` with
   no space around it — `पदविभागॱचिह्नक` 61 times, `सङ्केतनॱसङ्केत` 12 — and
   in type position `postfix_expr` is not available to spell them. `ॱ` is the
   frozen `member_mark`; whitespace around it is not significant, so these
   lex as three tokens, and `qualified_name` reads them.

   SECOND, WHAT WAS NOT ADDED HERE AND IS NOW SETTLED ABOVE. This note used
   to read "`पाठ` appears 145 times against `पाठः`'s 39, both in type position
   ... recorded here and NOT silently amended". ADR-0029 amended it on
   2026-08-31, after re-measuring by POSITION: 27 to 9, the other 148 being
   identifiers. `type_name` now spells `पाठ` and the lexicon follows.
   `अक्षरम्`, `प३२` and `प६४` are still frozen in `type_name` and written ZERO
   times; that is an unused spelling and not a divergence, so it is left
   alone — ADR-0027's reason, unchanged. *)

type           = type_name
               | slice_type
               | pointer_type
               | optional_type
               | error_union
               | qualified_name ;

qualified_name = identifier , { member_mark , identifier } ;


(* --- what is STILL not frozen, and why -------------------------------- *)

(* OPERATORS ARE NO LONGER HERE. This entry used to open "OPERATORS AND THEIR
 * PRECEDENCE. Doc 02 §3.2 writes them as ASCII `= + * →` … They need their own
 * row." That row was `D-002h`; ADR-0026 closed the question by MEASURING the
 * `.t1` corpus the owner ruled canonical, and the section above is frozen. The
 * entry is REPLACED rather than deleted, because a reader who remembers this
 * file as the place operators are open should find out here that they are not.
 *
 * `expression`, `statement`, `type` AND `call` ARE ALL DEFINED. This entry
 * used to say the last three were deferred non-terminals "defined nowhere";
 * ADR-0027 defined them, from the same corpus and by the same method
 * ADR-0026 used. THE HOLE THIS FILE HAS DESCRIBED SINCE B-079a IS CLOSED.
 *
 * WHAT IS STILL OPEN IS DECLARATION BODIES, and they are a different kind of
 * thing from what stood here — not a missing spelling, but a `आरभ्य … ऽ …
 * समाप्तम्` shape used by three constructs at once:
 *
 *   - `struct_body` and `enum_body`. `संरचना` (51) and `गणना` (3) both open
 *     with `आरभ्य` and separate with `ऽ`, exactly as `argument_list` does, so
 *     freezing them means saying how a declaration head is told from a call —
 *     which the KEYWORD already does, but it should be written down.
 *   - MATCH ARMS. No word for a match is written in the corpus at all, so
 *     there is nothing here to measure and this one is genuinely absent
 *     rather than merely unfrozen.
 *
 * AND ONE RECONCILIATION, which is `D-002h`'s remainder and is named in
 * ADR-0027 §"what this does not do": `spec/lexicon.src.tsv` still carries
 * `अधि`/`ऊन`/`गुण`/`भाग`/`विषमम्` as the operator words and
 * `crates/tree-sitter-t1/grammar.js` carries a third set with ASCII
 * `{ } ( ) ;` delimiters. Both are now WRONG against a frozen grammar rather
 * than divergent from an unfrozen one. `type_name`'s `पाठः` WAS the same
 * problem inside this file and is CLOSED: ADR-0029 froze `पाठ`, on 27 to 9
 * measured in type position rather than the 145 to 39 that counted every
 * occurrence of the word. What that ADR did NOT settle, and named on its way
 * past: `integer_type` is `( "अ" | "न" ) , type_width`, and the corpus writes
 * `इ६४` 56 times in type position while writing `न` — the frozen unsigned
 * prefix — ZERO times anywhere. That is a THIRD spelling this production
 * cannot produce, it is larger than the one just closed, and it is its own
 * decision.
 *
 * THAT DECISION IS TAKEN AND IT IS ADR-0030, WHICH ALSO CORRECTS THE SENTENCE
 * ABOVE: `न` is written ZERO times in the T1 corpus, not zero times ANYWHERE —
 * `spec/*.sas` writes it 123 times and `tools/gen_levels.py` 52 more, and the
 * conclusion the wider claim invited (that `इ` is the real signed prefix and
 * `अ` is not what the lexicon says) does not survive it. The production is NOT
 * widened; `इ६४` is a transliteration leak and `अ`-for-unsigned is the same
 * port defect running the other way. The sentence is kept as written rather
 * than edited in place, because a note that quietly acquires the right number
 * teaches nothing about how it got the wrong one.
 *)
